<p>Got a PM about this, took a look.</p>
<p>The first issue with this is that I have difficulty taking the Foundation response seriously. The researchers have no reason to believe that just because they found a location that produces anomalies, said anomalies will be automatically useful. It also makes me skeptical that they would immediately use said anomalies to assist the Foundation in the first place; the Foundation exists to contain anomalies first, not to put them to questionable/unreliable use. As a reader, I'm also confused as to how the researchers started running tests on the items in the first place, since the effects noted aren't really instinctive (for example, how would someone know which criteria to test to determine the specific effects of the box?).</p>
<p>&#8230;which brings me to the second major issue; I feel like there needs to be more explanation/worldbuilding behind <em>why</em> the warehouse behaves the way it does. I think we already have a Thaumiel location that knowingly assists the Foundation (I think it's a renovated schoolbuilding or the like; try using the <a href="/tag-search">Tag Search</a> tool to find it), so what you have here just seems like a slight variation of it but without narrative context. Did the warehouse just spontaneously start existing to produce anomalies? Why does it want to help the Foundation at all?</p>
<p>Also, last thing, note the difference between sentient and sapient. Sentience can be attributed to animals like dogs and cats and lobsters that can perceive and react to external stimuli and feel pain; sapience is what is needed for human-level self-awareness and communication ability.</p>
<p>Overall&#8230; I'm not convinced that this would be workable on the mainsite as currently presented. The Foundation personnel seem too quickly disappointed, and the warehouse itself seems shallowly developed. I think the first steps to take here would be to better develop the warehouse's history and motives so it exists as a meaningful character on its own, without needing the Foundation to give it significance.</p>

							</div>

								
					
