			<div class="content" id="post-content-4850635">
									

<p>Took a look at the revised version. It's still too contrivedly overpowered for me to take seriously, especially given that the creator individual somehow knows so much about the Foundation without any solid in-universe reasoning to explain their knowledge. The fact that they just happen to have the means to produce anomalies that could be Thaumiel class (how would they know what Thaumiel class is? Why would they assume Foundation personnel would trust their products, let alone pay for them?) and yet their scheme is disturbed by something as mundane as a coal company just makes the entire story seem sloppily set up.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>Material like this:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>They found out about the foundation after witnessing them contain an SCP without getting noticed himself (thorough as the foundation might be) and then began to educate himself on how to make anomalies (not yet specified, more research needed).</p>
</blockquote>
<p>just makes me as a reader skeptical. It's already a stretch of disbelief for me to believe that the Foundation just happened to not notice this person witnessing a containment incident (<em>and</em> said person just <em>happens</em> to have the factory means to produce anomalies somehow). When you add in the fact that they somehow learned enough about the Foundation to figure out the kind of anomalies that would be considered useful, and <em>also</em> created a program that can &quot;crack open many cybersecurity features (not all) and then infect every possible device that can print&quot;, the result is a story that just feels too forced by authorial meddling to read as genuine or believable.</p>
<p>Why do you want to write about a factory that fails to make certain kinds of anomalies? Why Thaumiel-class items, specifically? Why do you want the factory to be the work of one person? Why do you want them to just randomly and conveniently find out a lot of information about the Foundation, thereby making the Foundation look incompetent in terms of information security?</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>I feel like so far you've set a lot of difficult challenges up for the story, seemingly for no other reason than you just happen to want to write things that way rather than the narrative organically developing to include those details. Most of these details are problematic because they make the story seem too fake or the main character and their creations too overpowered. How open are you to changing these elements to make the story more believable and the main character more of a relatable individual and not an excessively exceptional caricature?</p>

							</div>

								
					
