			<div class="content" id="post-content-4850714">
									

<p>Thank you for taking a look at my concept yet again.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>My answers:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>how would they know what Thaumiel class is?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>They don't. They simply try to manufacture Objects that could aid the foundation, who would then in turn be classified as Thaumiel.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Why would they assume Foundation personnel would trust their products</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is the reason this individual does not speak to the foundation at all while building the factory. They want the products to speak for themselves.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>let alone pay for them?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Since this concept would have been to long if I included details about the individual i left them out. This individual inherits a vast amount of money from their dead family members. This money will be used to set everything necessary up. They have a genuine good heart and witnesses the foundation helping humans against an anomaly. Since he is not directly from the foundation he does not know of the inner workings and events, i.e. he thinks that the foundation in its entirety is completely good and would never do anything evil.<br />
With that in mind, he tries to fabricate objects that would help them while also receiving money in return for his work. He doesn't even think about the foundation ceasing his property. Since his family is quite rich he has always been taught that money is his highest priority and he follows those teachings.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>It's already a stretch of disbelief for me to believe that the Foundation just happened to not notice this person witnessing a containment incident</p>
</blockquote>
<p>That makes sense, but I also find it hard to believe that the foundation catches every single person that witnesses a containment incident. Someone will slip by somehow eventually but this time it's a nice person.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>and said person just happens to have the factory means to produce anomalies somehow</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This Person does not happen to have it. He builds it himself. How he does that is something I am still working on. Plan is currently that he uses his money to hire people that know a thing or two and compiles their knowledge.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>When you add in the fact that they somehow learned enough about the Foundation to figure out the kind of anomalies that would be considered useful</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I have thought about that part. Since he himself would be confronted with anomalies or at least some knowledge of them he would know some dangers, like looking at them or directly touching them. He is building a abnormal factory after all. So the stuff he would produce are things he thinks are useful like gloves that make your hands indestructable, boxes that lock things inside with no way of opening them from the inside, glasses that protect your mind from seeing things and hazmat suits to keep nasty stuff away.</p>
<p>Since this individual has knowledge on how to construct a factory and knowledge of the abnormal, I dont think this is too far fetched. Also he has no direct contact to the foundation. That is the reason he programs a virus that randomly infects printers etc. to print that messages because he knows the foundation will pick up the signal and find him. He has not done so sooner because he wanted his products to be finished before contacting them.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Why do you want the factory to be the work of one person?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I want the factory to have 1 important person in charge, who hired other people to help him build it, yet him pulling all the strings.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Why do you want them to just randomly and conveniently find out a lot of information about the Foundation, thereby making the Foundation look incompetent in terms of information security?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I dont want to do that. I want 1 single mistake from the foundation and 1 single rich person with a good heart and a too naive way of thinking to clash and culminate into this story.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>their scheme is disturbed by something as mundane as a coal company</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The coal company part is definetely something that could get changed, but I don't think it is huge problem. The individual needs money to build up his little factory and the coal company makes prices go up by buying all needed materials in the area, thereby making it more expensive for him to buy said materials. Also, he still is a good person that abides by the rules and just ignores it. His money eventually runs low and he begins to sell businesses that his family own that gave him money in the first place in order to fullfill his current dream. He risks it all and gets nothing because he is too naive and simple minded.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>How open are you to changing these elements to make the story more believable and the main character more of a relatable individual and not an excessively exceptional caricature?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I am open to any changes that make the story better/clearer without completely changing its direction.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I feel like so far you've set a lot of difficult challenges up for the story</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I want this story to work and am willing to put in as much effort as needed by me. That being said, I really should make a draft before saying that.</p>
